 "grammar": {
  "title": {
   "ko": "현재완료 have / has + 과거분사",
   "en": "Present perfect: have / has + past participle"
  },
  "explain": {
   "ko": "과거에 일어났지만 지금과 연결되는 경험은 현재완료(have / has + 과거분사)로 말합니다. 경험을 물을 때는 Have you ever + 과거분사?를 씁니다. 한 번도 없으면 never, 이미 했으면 already, 아직 안 했으면 yet(부정문·의문문)을 씁니다. 규칙 동사의 과거분사는 과거형과 같고(visited), 불규칙은 따로 외웁니다(go → been / gone, eat → eaten, see → seen).",
   "en": "Use the present perfect (have / has + past participle) for experiences up to now when the exact time is not important. Ask about experience with 'Have you ever + past participle?'. Use never (not once), already (done before now) and yet (in negatives and questions). Regular past participles look like the past simple (visited); irregular ones must be learnt (go → been / gone, eat → eaten, see → seen)."
  },
  "table": {
   "head": [
    "동사 / Verb",
    "과거형 / Past",
    "과거분사 / Past participle"
   ],
   "rows": [
    [
     "be",
     "was / were",
     "been"
    ],
    [
     "go",
     "went",
     "gone / been"
    ],
    [
     "see",
     "saw",
     "seen"
    ],
    [
     "eat",
     "ate",
     "eaten"
    ],
    [
     "visit",
     "visited",
     "visited"
    ]
   ]
  },
  "notes": [
   {
    "ko": "정확한 때가 있으면 과거형: I went to Paris in 2020.",
    "en": "With a finished time use the past simple: I went to Paris in 2020."
   },
   {
    "ko": "been to는 '가 본 적이 있다', gone to는 '가고 지금 없다'입니다.",
    "en": "'Been to' means has visited and come back; 'gone to' means is there now."
   },
   {
    "ko": "he / she / it 뒤에는 has: She has been to Japan.",
    "en": "With he / she / it use has: She has been to Japan."
   }
  ],
  "examples": [
   {
